@meta
id: 16-01
title: A tab is not a file
chapter: 16
type: lesson
skills: buffer-list, buffer-switch
par: 50
minutes: 5
vscode: three tabs along the top; click the third one
open: order.entity.ts errors.json order.repository.ts

@brief
Three files open, and the screen shows one of them. In VS Code the other
two would be tabs along the top. Here they are buffers: files vim has
loaded, whether or not any of them is on screen. What you are looking at
is a window, a viewport onto one buffer. The buffer is the real thing.
The window is a view of it. A tab, when we get there, is a layout of
windows. This chapter is about keeping those three apart, and it starts
with the list.

`:ls` prints every buffer vim has: a number, some flags, the name, and
the line the cursor was on last. Something like

    1 %a   "order.entity.ts"       line 1
    2      "errors.json"           line 0
    3      "order.repository.ts"   line 0

`%` marks the buffer in the current window and `a` says it is active,
meaning shown somewhere. `line 0` means vim has not even read the file
yet; it opens them lazily. The number on the left is the buffer's
address for the rest of the chapter.

The job is in `order.repository.ts`. `toEntity` copies every field of
the record except `total`. Add `total: record.total,` directly under
the `status` line. The other two files are open for context only, and
the check fails if either of them changes.

@keys
| `:ls` | list the buffers (`:buffers` and `:files` do the same) |
| `:b 3` | go to buffer 3 |
| `:bn` `:bp` | next and previous buffer in the list |
| `:wqa` | write every changed buffer and quit |

@hints
List first. The repository is buffer 3, not 2, because it was opened
third. Nothing about the order on screen tells you that; the list does.
--
`:b 3` goes straight to it. `:bn` twice also works and is the long way
round.
--
In the repository, `/status` lands on the line, `o` opens one below it,
type the field, `Esc`, then `:wqa`.

@solution
    :ls<Enter>  :b 3<Enter>  /status<Enter>  o total: record.total, <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

Around forty keystrokes, and the only new ones are `:ls` and `:b 3`.

The model to carry through the chapter: a buffer is a loaded file, a
window is a view of a buffer, and a tab is an arrangement of windows.
VS Code fuses the first two into "a tab", which is why it feels natural
to have thirty of them open and to click along the row looking for the
right one. Vim never asks you to look. `:ls` tells you the number, `:b`
takes the number, and the screen does not need to change until you say
so.

`:wqa` has been in every multi-file lesson so far and you were told to
use it without explanation. Now you can read it: write all changed
buffers, on screen or not, then quit all windows. Its two halves, `:wa`
and `:qa`, turn up again in 16-06.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}
